; lean under on his rushing line.</p><p><strong>Brady Cook</strong> has a passing-yards tailwind \u2014 DET allow <strong>249 pass yds/game</strong> (vs ~230).</p><p>Sneaky value on <strong>Adonai Mitchell</strong> (WR2) \u2014 DET leak 45 yds/game to the WR2 role, above the ~36 norm.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Breece Hall</strong> is a bell-cow (66% of carries) meeting a defense that allows 0.63 RB rushing TDs/game \u2014 strong anytime-TD equity.</p><p><strong>Mason Taylor</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 DET are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.29 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p>In the red zone NYJ throw it (61% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Garrett Wilson</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>Fade the NYJ run \u2014 as 10-point dogs projected to chase, <strong>Breece Hall</strong>'s carries get squeezed, though his receiving work ticks up in catch-up mode.</p><p>A middling 46 total \u2014 no strong lean either way; the value is in the individual matchups above, not the game environment.</p><p><strong>Indoors</strong> \u2014 a dome removes weather variance and historically nudges passing efficiency up; a small tailwind for the passing games.</p><h3>DET \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>DET are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (42% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>Jahmyr Gibbs</strong> (13 carries, 70 yards projected) gets a mild game-script tailwind as a favorite, though DET's willingness to keep throwing when ahead caps how much the lead converts into extra carries.</p><p><strong>Amon-Ra St. Brown</strong> projects for <strong>8 targets and 72 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Sam LaPorta</strong> (TE, 5 targets, 42 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>NYJ \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>NYJ are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (45% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 NYJ project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Breece Hall</strong>'s rushing projection (13 carries, 57 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Garrett Wilson</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, NYJ funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>7 targets for 51 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Mason Taylor</strong> (TE, 5 targets, 30 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 10-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather